 *
 * The owner, 2026-10-19: "transplant [the ZeroStick calibration] feature and
 * calibration workflow into the ESP32-P4. In Controller Aim, at the bottom,
 * a button you click - 'Calibration' - opens a sub-menu with that
 * calibration UI and functions, so you can calibrate your controller (PS5),
 * and include my 8K controller."
 *
 * WHAT IT IS
 *   The Calibration button at the foot of Controller Aim opens one screen
 *   (back key "‹ Aim"). The Core says which pad is on its controller port
 *   (GET /padcal/pad) and lends it to this screen (POST /padcal/open): from
 *   then until the screen closes, the console does not see that pad at all
 *   (the Core tells the console it let go, then forwards nothing), so
 *   spinning the sticks and pressing Cross to confirm never reach a game.
 *
 *   A DualSense, DualSense Edge or DualShock 4 gets the ZS Pad Calibrator,
 *   1:1: the dualshock-tools site (MIT, (c) 2024 the_al) with the owner's
 *   hotkeys and auto-save, vendored whole in vendor/dualshock-tools/ and run
 *   in a frame. Its WebHID is zs-core-hid.js, which reaches the pad through
 *   ZSPadCalBridge below and the Core's /padcal/... routes. The flow, its
 *   steps and its timings are the site's own; nothing here changes them.
 *
 *   A ZS 8K gets its own calibration (the dualshock-tools clone checks
 *   would rightly refuse it): the ZS8K Configurator's wizard, ported with
 *   its constants (zs8k-cal.js has the numbers), through the 8K's setup
 *   port (/padcal/zs8k/...). Circle calibrates the sticks and Cross saves (the PlayStation
 *   flow's hotkeys); range and fine-tune open only from the app (owner,
 *   2026-10-21); the d-pad nudges inside fine-tune.
 *
 *   The pad goes back to the console when the screen closes (back key, or
 *   any other way: a watcher sees the screen gone and closes), and by itself
 *   15 s after the app goes quiet (the Core's lease).
 *
 * ES5 (DESIGN.md). Load after zs8k-cal.js.
 */
